var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"man/codata/#man-codata","page":"CODATA Releases","text":"The CODATA task group on fundamental constants publishes updated recommended values every four years.  AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.jl ships constants from every release since 2002 and lets you choose which one to use at runtime.","title":"CODATA Releases"},{"category":"section","location":"man/codata/#Supported-releases","page":"CODATA Releases","text":"Release Notes\n2002 earliest supported; M_TRITON is not tabulated in this release (CODATA2002.M_TRITON == NaN)\n2006 \n2010 first release with G_HELION_NUCLEAR, ANOMALY_ELECTRON, ANOMALY_MUON\n2014 \n2018 \n2022 default","title":"Supported releases"},{"category":"section","location":"man/codata/#Checking-the-active-release","page":"CODATA Releases","text":"using AtomicAndPhysicalConstants\nRELEASE_YEAR   # Int – the currently active release year","title":"Checking the active release"},{"category":"section","location":"man/codata/#Changing-the-release","page":"CODATA Releases","text":"using AtomicAndPhysicalConstants\n\nset_release(year = \"2014\")\n# [ Info: The default CODATA release is now 2014.\n#         Restart your Julia session for this change to take effect.\n\nThe setting is stored via Preferences.jl and persists across Julia sessions until changed again.\n\nnote: Restart required\nConstants are resolved at package load time, so a Julia session restart is required for the new release to take effect.\n\nwarning: Unsupported releases\nOnly the years listed under Supported releases above are valid. If the stored preference points at an unsupported release (for example after hand-editing LocalPreferences.toml), the package raises an error at load time listing the valid options rather than silently falling back to the default.\n\nTo revert to the default (2022):\n\nset_release()          # no argument → defaults to \"2022\"","title":"Changing the release"},{"category":"section","location":"man/codata/#Direct-access-to-release-structs","page":"CODATA Releases","text":"Each release is also exported as a named CODATA_release struct. You can inspect or use individual values without changing the global default:\n\nCODATA2018.M_ELECTRON    # electron mass from the 2018 release\nCODATA2014.C_LIGHT       # speed of light from the 2014 release","title":"Direct access to release structs"},{"category":"section","location":"man/codata/#Constants-not-in-CODATA","page":"CODATA Releases","text":"The pion masses (M_PION_0, M_PION_CHARGED) are not published in any CODATA release.  They are taken from Particle Data Group (PDG) tables and remain constant regardless of the selected release year.\n\nCLASSICAL_RADIUS_FACTOR is derived rather than tabulated: it is computed as R_ELECTRON * M_ELECTRON from the active release.  It is therefore not a field of the CODATA_release structs — CODATA2018.CLASSICAL_RADIUS_FACTOR does not exist, only the exported top-level constant.","title":"Constants not in CODATA"},{"category":"section","location":"man/codata/#Constants-that-differ-from-the-tabulated-release-value","page":"CODATA Releases","text":"G_DEUTERON, G_HELION, and G_TRITON are pulled from the NIST/CODATA tables but are rescaled before export.  NIST tabulates these g-factors against the nuclear magneton mu_N = ehbar2m_p; the package converts them to the particle's own magneton so that the gyromagnetic anomaly a = (g-2)2 comes out correct:\n\nG_DEUTERON == CODATA2022.G_DEUTERON_NUCLEAR * M_DEUTERON / M_PROTON   # active release\n\nThe release structs name these fields G_DEUTERON_NUCLEAR, G_HELION_NUCLEAR, and G_TRITON_NUCLEAR — the suffix flags the nuclear-magneton normalization, so there is no field whose name matches the exported constant and whose value differs from it:\n\nCODATA2022.G_DEUTERON_NUCLEAR   # 0.8574382335  — as published by NIST\nCODATA2022.G_DEUTERON           # ERROR: no such field\nG_DEUTERON                      # 1.7140254606… — renormalized for a = (g-2)/2\n\nBoth the mass and the g-factor come from the active release, so the conversion stays self-consistent when you call set_release.  The active release's unscaled values are also kept as the unexported _G_DEUTERON, _G_HELION, and _G_TRITON.  See Physical Constants for the full explanation.\n\nThe other g-factors (G_ELECTRON, G_MUON, G_PROTON, G_NEUTRON) are exported exactly as tabulated.","title":"Constants that differ from the tabulated release value"},{"category":"section","location":"man/codata/#Release-coverage-caveats","page":"CODATA Releases","text":"The gyromagnetic anomalies (ANOMALY_ELECTRON, ANOMALY_MUON) and the helion g-factor (G_HELION_NUCLEAR) were not individually tabulated by CODATA until the 2010 release.  Using a pre-2010 release will still define these symbols; worth verifying their values against the source data if you are doing precision work prior to that release.","title":"Release coverage caveats"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"Complete index of all exported symbols.","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Internal-Types","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Internal Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Species-type-and-constructor","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Species type and constructor"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Accessor-functions","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Accessor functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Configuration","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Particle-kind-enum","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Particle kind enum"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Particle-data-dictionaries","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Particle data dictionaries"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#CODATA-release-structs","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"CODATA release structs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Physical-constants","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Physical constants"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.SubatomicSpecies","page":"API Reference","text":"SubatomicSpecies\n\nInternal struct storing intrinsic data for a single subatomic particle. Instances are stored in SUBATOMIC_SPECIES.\n\nFields\n\nspeciesname::String — openPMD particle identifier.\ncharge::Float64 — charge in units of e.\nmass::Float64 — mass in eV/c².\nmoment::Float64 — magnetic dipole moment in eV/T.\nspin::Float64 — spin in ħ.\ngspin::Float64 — spin g-factor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.SubatomicSpecies"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.AtomicSpecies","page":"API Reference","text":"AtomicSpecies\n\nInternal struct storing isotope mass data for a chemical element. Instances are stored in ATOMIC_SPECIES.\n\nFields\n\nZ::Int — atomic number (number of protons).\nspeciesname::String — standard atomic symbol (e.g. \"Fe\").\nmass::Dict{Int,Float64} — isotope masses in atomic mass units (u), keyed by mass number.  The special key −1 holds the abundance-averaged atomic mass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.AtomicSpecies"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CODATA_release","page":"API Reference","text":"CODATA_release\n\nKeyword-argument struct holding all fundamental constants from one CODATA release year.  Fields mirror the exported scalar constants (M_ELECTRON, C_LIGHT, etc.) but are grouped together so that multiple release years can coexist in memory simultaneously.\n\nPre-built instances are exported as CODATA2002, CODATA2006, CODATA2010, CODATA2014, CODATA2018, and CODATA2022.\n\nCODATA2018.M_ELECTRON   # electron mass from the 2018 release\nCODATA2014.C_LIGHT      # speed of light from the 2014 release\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CODATA_release"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.Species","page":"API Reference","text":"Species(speciesname::String)\nSpecies()\n\nConstruct a particle species by name, or a null placeholder with no arguments.\n\nThe string format encodes particle identity, isotope (for atoms), and charge state in one expression: [mass_number] symbol [charge].\n\nSubatomic particles\n\nPass the openPMD name exactly:\n\nName Particle\n\"electron\" / \"positron\" electron / positron\n\"proton\" / \"anti-proton\" proton / antiproton\n\"neutron\" / \"anti-neutron\" neutron / antineutron\n\"muon\" / \"anti-muon\" muon / antimuon\n\"pion0\" / \"pion+\" / \"pion-\" pions\n\"deuteron\" / \"anti-deuteron\" deuteron / antideuteron\n\"photon\" photon\n\nAtomic species\n\nAtomic symbols \"H\" (Z=1) through \"Og\" (Z=118) are supported.\n\nMass number — There are two ways to include the mass number: Before the atomic symbol, either prefix with a pound symbol # followed by the mass number or, prefix with a  Unicode superscript(s). A bare ASCII mass number (e.g. \"4He\") is not accepted. Correct would be \"#4He\" or \"⁴He\".\n\nCharge state — append after the symbol. Repeated signs (\"++\", \"---\") or \"+n\" / \"-n\" notation are both accepted.\n\nSpecies(\"#4He\")     # helium-4, neutral (# prefix required for ASCII digits)\nSpecies(\"⁴He\")      # same, Unicode superscript\nSpecies(\"Li+3\")     # lithium, charge +3\nSpecies(\"Li+++\")    # same\nSpecies(\"K-2\")      # potassium, charge −2\n\nAnti-atoms: prepend \"anti-\" to any atomic symbol.\n\nSpecies(\"anti-H\")   # antihydrogen\n\nNull species: Species(), or names \"Null\", \"null\", \"\".\n\nFields\n\nField Type Description\nname String Particle name or atomic symbol\ncharge Float64 Net charge in units of e\nmass Float64 Rest mass in eV/c²\nspin Float64 Spin in ħ\ngspin Float64 Spin g-factor (0 for atoms)\nmoment Float64 Magnetic dipole moment in eV/T (0 for atoms)\niso Int Mass number; −1 for abundance average; 0 for subatomic particles\nkind Kind.T LEPTON, HADRON, PHOTON, ATOM, or NULL\n\nDirect field access is disabled. Use chargeof, massof, spinof, g_spin, momentof, iso_of, atomicnumberof, kindof, isnullspecies, Base.nameof.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.Species"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.nameof-Tuple{Species}","page":"API Reference","text":"nameof(species::Species) -> String\n\nReturn the canonical name of species.\n\nFor subatomic particles the openPMD name is returned unchanged. For atomic species the name is assembled from the atomic symbol, mass number (if a specific isotope was requested), and charge state, using the #mAS±c convention:\n\nnameof(Species(\"electron\"))   # \"electron\"\nnameof(Species(\"Fe\"))         # \"Fe\"\nnameof(Species(\"#4He+2\"))     # \"#4He+2\"\nnameof(Species(\"Li+\"))        # \"Li+1\"\nnameof(Species(\"anti-#4He\"))  # \"anti-#4He\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.nameof"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.chargeof","page":"API Reference","text":"chargeof(species::Species; C::Bool = false) -> Float64\n\nReturn the net charge of species.\n\nBy default the charge is returned as a (whole-numbered) multiple of the elementary charge e.  Pass C = true to convert to coulombs using the active E_CHARGE constant.\n\nExamples\n\nchargeof(Species(\"proton\"))       # 1.0\nchargeof(Species(\"electron\"))     # -1.0\nchargeof(Species(\"Li+3\"))         # 3.0\nchargeof(Species(\"proton\"), C=true)   # ≈ 1.602176634e-19\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.chargeof"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.massof","page":"API Reference","text":"massof(species::Species; AMU::Bool = false) -> Float64\n\nReturn the rest mass of species.\n\nBy default the mass is returned in eV/c².  Pass AMU = true to return the mass in atomic mass units (daltons), which is particularly convenient for atomic species.\n\nExamples\n\nmassof(Species(\"electron\"))              # 510998.95069  eV/c²\nmassof(Species(\"proton\"))               # 9.38272089430e8  eV/c²\nmassof(Species(\"H\"), AMU = true)        # ≈ 1.00794  u\nmassof(Species(\"#4He\"), AMU = true)     # ≈ 4.0026  u\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.massof"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.spinof","page":"API Reference","text":"spinof(species::Species) -> Float64\n\nReturn the spin of species in units of the reduced Planck constant ħ.\n\nExamples\n\nspinof(Species(\"electron\"))    # 0.5\nspinof(Species(\"proton\"))      # 0.5\nspinof(Species(\"photon\"))      # 1.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.spinof"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.g_spin","page":"API Reference","text":"g_spin(species::Species; signed::Bool = false) -> Float64\n\nReturn the spin g-factor of species.\n\nBy default the absolute value is returned.  Pass signed = true to get the signed g-factor (negative for particles with a negative gyromagnetic ratio, such as the electron).\n\nReturns 0 for atomic species, for which no g-factor is stored.\n\ngyromagnetic_anomaly is built on the default, unsigned value.\n\nExamples\n\ng_spin(Species(\"electron\"))               # 2.00231930436092\ng_spin(Species(\"electron\"), signed=true)  # -2.00231930436092\ng_spin(Species(\"proton\"))                 # 5.5856946893\ng_spin(Species(\"H\"))                      # 0.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.g_spin"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.gyromagnetic_anomaly","page":"API Reference","text":"gyromagnetic_anomaly(species::Species) -> Float64\n\nCompute and return the gyromagnetic anomaly\n\na = fracg - 22\n\nfor leptons and hadrons.  Returns NaN for photons, atoms, and null species, since the gyromagnetic anomaly is not defined for them.\n\nThe unsigned g-factor is used, i.e. g_spin is called with its default signed = false.  The sign of the stored g-factor records the orientation of the magnetic moment relative to the spin, not the size of the anomaly, so species whose g-factor is negative (electron, muon, neutron, helion) still get the conventional positive anomaly: the electron gives +0.00115965…, not -2.0011….\n\nThe stored g-factors are all in the convention mu = g * (e / 2m) * S, where the particle's own mass sets the magneton, so this formula applies uniformly.  For the deuteron, helion, and triton this required renormalizing the NIST values, which are tabulated against the nuclear magneton e*hbar/(2*M_PROTON); G_DEUTERON, G_HELION, and G_TRITON carry the renormalized values (see the Physical Constants manual page).\n\nExamples\n\ngyromagnetic_anomaly(Species(\"electron\"))   # ≈  0.00115965218046\ngyromagnetic_anomaly(Species(\"muon\"))       # ≈  0.00116592062\ngyromagnetic_anomaly(Species(\"neutron\"))    # ≈  0.91304276\ngyromagnetic_anomaly(Species(\"deuteron\"))   # ≈ -0.1429872697\ngyromagnetic_anomaly(Species(\"H\"))          # NaN\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.gyromagnetic_anomaly"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.momentof","page":"API Reference","text":"momentof(species::Species) -> Float64\n\nReturn the magnetic dipole moment of species in eV/T.\n\nReturns 0 for atomic species and the null species (no moment is stored for these types).\n\nExamples\n\nmomentof(Species(\"electron\"))   # ≈ -5.795094307320036e-5  eV/T\nmomentof(Species(\"proton\"))     # ≈  8.8043151136e-8  eV/T\nmomentof(Species(\"H\"))          # 0.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.momentof"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.iso_of","page":"API Reference","text":"iso_of(species::Species) -> Int\n\nReturn the mass number (isotope) of species.\n\nFor atomic species: the mass number of the requested isotope, or −1 if the abundance-averaged atomic mass was used.\nFor subatomic particles: always 0.\n\nExamples\n\niso_of(Species(\"#3He\"))         # 3\niso_of(Species(\"He\"))           # -1  (abundance average)\niso_of(Species(\"electron\"))     # 0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.iso_of"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.atomicnumberof","page":"API Reference","text":"atomicnumberof(species::Species) -> Int\n\nReturn the atomic number (number of protons) of species.\n\nFor an anti-atom, returns the negative of the atomic number (-Z), to distinguish it from the matter atom of the same symbol.\n\nThrows an error if species is not of kind ATOM.\n\nExamples\n\natomicnumberof(Species(\"Fe\"))       # 26\natomicnumberof(Species(\"H+\"))       # 1\natomicnumberof(Species(\"anti-H\"))   # -1\natomicnumberof(Species(\"electron\")) # ERROR\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.atomicnumberof"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.kindof","page":"API Reference","text":"kindof(species::Species) -> Kind.T\n\nReturn the particle classification of species as a Kind.T enum value.\n\nPossible values: Kind.LEPTON, Kind.HADRON, Kind.PHOTON, Kind.ATOM, Kind.NULL.\n\nExamples\n\nkindof(Species(\"electron\"))    # Kind.LEPTON\nkindof(Species(\"proton\"))      # Kind.HADRON\nkindof(Species(\"H\"))           # Kind.ATOM\nkindof(Species(\"photon\"))      # Kind.PHOTON\nkindof(Species())              # Kind.NULL\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.kindof"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.isnullspecies","page":"API Reference","text":"isnullspecies(species::Species) -> Bool\n\nReturn true if species is a null (placeholder) species, false otherwise.\n\nisnullspecies(Species())           # true\nisnullspecies(Species(\"null\"))     # true\nisnullspecies(Species(\"proton\"))   # false\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.isnullspecies"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.set_release","page":"API Reference","text":"set_release(; year::String = \"2022\")\n\nPersistently set the CODATA release year used by AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.jl.\n\nThe setting is stored via Preferences.jl in the active Julia environment and survives across sessions.  A Julia restart is required for the new constants to take effect.\n\nValid values for year: \"2002\", \"2006\", \"2010\", \"2014\", \"2018\", \"2022\".  Calling with no arguments resets to the default (\"2022\").\n\nExamples\n\nset_release(year = \"2014\")\n# [ Info: The default CODATA release is now 2014.\n#         Restart your Julia session for this change to take effect.\n\nset_release()   # revert to 2022\n\nSee also: RELEASE_YEAR.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.set_release"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.Kind","page":"API Reference","text":"Kind\n\nClassification of a particle species.\n\nValue Meaning\nKind.LEPTON leptons: electron, positron, muon, anti-muon\nKind.HADRON hadrons: proton, neutron, pions, deuteron, …\nKind.PHOTON photon\nKind.ATOM any atomic or ionic species\nKind.NULL null placeholder species\n\nThe kind of a species is queried with kindof.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.Kind"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.SUBATOMIC_SPECIES","page":"API Reference","text":"SUBATOMIC_SPECIES :: Dict{String, SubatomicSpecies}\n\nDictionary of all supported subatomic particles, keyed by openPMD name.\n\nSupported keys: \"electron\", \"positron\", \"proton\", \"anti-proton\", \"neutron\", \"anti-neutron\", \"muon\", \"anti-muon\", \"pion0\", \"pion+\", \"pion-\", \"deuteron\", \"anti-deuteron\", \"triton\", \"anti-triton\", \"helion\", \"anti-helion\", \"photon\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.SUBATOMIC_SPECIES"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.ATOMIC_SPECIES","page":"API Reference","text":"ATOMIC_SPECIES :: Dict{String, AtomicSpecies}\n\nDictionary of all supported atomic elements (Z = 1 … 118), keyed by atomic symbol (e.g. \"H\", \"Fe\", \"Og\").  Each value is an AtomicSpecies struct containing the atomic number, symbol, and a dictionary of isotope masses in atomic mass units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.ATOMIC_SPECIES"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CODATA2002","page":"API Reference","text":"CODATA2002 :: CODATA_release\n\nFundamental constants from the 2002 CODATA release (earliest supported).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CODATA2002"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CODATA2006","page":"API Reference","text":"CODATA2006 :: CODATA_release\n\nFundamental constants from the 2006 CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CODATA2006"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CODATA2010","page":"API Reference","text":"CODATA2010 :: CODATA_release\n\nFundamental constants from the 2010 CODATA release. First release to include G_HELION_NUCLEAR, ANOMALY_ELECTRON, and ANOMALY_MUON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CODATA2010"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CODATA2014","page":"API Reference","text":"CODATA2014 :: CODATA_release\n\nFundamental constants from the 2014 CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CODATA2014"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CODATA2018","page":"API Reference","text":"CODATA2018 :: CODATA_release\n\nFundamental constants from the 2018 CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CODATA2018"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CODATA2022","page":"API Reference","text":"CODATA2022 :: CODATA_release\n\nFundamental constants from the 2022 CODATA release. This is the default release used when no preference has been set.\n\nSee NIST 2022 CODATA for the primary source values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CODATA2022"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_ELECTRON","page":"API Reference","text":"M_ELECTRON::Float64 - Mass of the electron in eV/c^2 from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_ELECTRON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_PROTON","page":"API Reference","text":"M_PROTON::Float64 - Mass of the proton in eV/c^2 from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_PROTON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_NEUTRON","page":"API Reference","text":"M_NEUTRON::Float64 - Mass of the neutron in eV/c^2 from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_NEUTRON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_MUON","page":"API Reference","text":"M_MUON::Float64 - Mass of the muon in eV/c^2 from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_MUON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_DEUTERON","page":"API Reference","text":"M_DEUTERON::Float64 - Mass of the deuteron in eV/c^2 from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_DEUTERON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_HELION","page":"API Reference","text":"M_HELION::Float64 - Mass of the helion in eV/c^2 from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_HELION"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_TRITON","page":"API Reference","text":"M_TRITON::Float64 - Mass of the triton in eV/c^2 from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_TRITON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_PION_0","page":"API Reference","text":"MPION0::Float64 - Mass of the neutral pion in eV/c^2 scraped from the particle data group.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_PION_0"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_PION_CHARGED","page":"API Reference","text":"MPIONCHARGED::Float64 - Mass of a charged pion in eV/c^2 scraped from the particle data group.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.M_PION_CHARGED"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_ELECTRON","page":"API Reference","text":"MU_ELECTRON::Float64 - Magnetic moment of the electron in eV/T (converted) from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_ELECTRON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_PROTON","page":"API Reference","text":"MU_PROTON::Float64 - Magnetic moment of the proton in eV/T (converted) from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_PROTON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_NEUTRON","page":"API Reference","text":"MU_NEUTRON::Float64 - Magnetic moment of the neutron in eV/T (converted) from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_NEUTRON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_MUON","page":"API Reference","text":"MU_MUON::Float64 - Magnetic moment of the muon in eV/T (converted) from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_MUON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_DEUTERON","page":"API Reference","text":"MU_DEUTERON::Float64 - Magnetic moment of the deuteron in eV/T (converted) from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_DEUTERON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_HELION","page":"API Reference","text":"MU_HELION::Float64 - Magnetic moment of the helion in eV/T (converted) from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_HELION"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_TRITON","page":"API Reference","text":"MU_TRITON::Float64 - Magnetic moment of the triton in eV/T (converted) from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_TRITON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_ELECTRON","page":"API Reference","text":"G_ELECTRON::Float64 - The electron spin g-factor per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_ELECTRON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_PROTON","page":"API Reference","text":"G_PROTON::Float64 - The proton spin g-factor per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_PROTON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_NEUTRON","page":"API Reference","text":"G_NEUTRON::Float64 - The neutron spin g-factor per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_NEUTRON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_MUON","page":"API Reference","text":"G_MUON::Float64 - The muon spin g-factor per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_MUON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_DEUTERON","page":"API Reference","text":"GDEUTERON::Float64 - The deuteron spin g-factor renormalized to the deuteron magneton `ehbar/(2MDEUTERON), the convention in which the gyromagnetic anomalya = (|g|-2)/2` holds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_DEUTERON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_HELION","page":"API Reference","text":"GHELION::Float64 - The helion spin g-factor renormalized to the helion magneton `ehbar/(2MHELION), the convention in which the gyromagnetic anomalya = (|g|-2)/2` holds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_HELION"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_TRITON","page":"API Reference","text":"GTRITON::Float64 - The triton spin g-factor renormalized to the triton magneton `ehbar/(2MTRITON), the convention in which the gyromagnetic anomalya = (|g|-2)/2` holds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_TRITON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.ANOMALY_ELECTRON","page":"API Reference","text":"ANOMALY_ELECTRON::Float64 - The electron gyromagnetic anomaly per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.ANOMALY_ELECTRON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.ANOMALY_MUON","page":"API Reference","text":"ANOMALY_MUON::Float64 - The muon gyromagnetic anomaly per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.ANOMALY_MUON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.E_CHARGE","page":"API Reference","text":"E_CHARGE::Float64 - magnitude of charge on the electron in C per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.E_CHARGE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.C_LIGHT","page":"API Reference","text":"C_LIGHT::Float64 - Speed of light in m/s per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.C_LIGHT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.H_PLANCK","page":"API Reference","text":"H_PLANCK::Float64 - Planck's constant (h) in eV*s per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.H_PLANCK"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.H_BAR","page":"API Reference","text":"H_BAR::Float64 - Planck's reduced constant (ħ) in eV*s per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.H_BAR"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.R_ELECTRON","page":"API Reference","text":"R_ELECTRON::Float64 - Classical electron radius in m per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.R_ELECTRON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.R_PROTON","page":"API Reference","text":"R_PROTON::Float64 - Classical proton radius in m per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.R_PROTON"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CLASSICAL_RADIUS_FACTOR","page":"API Reference","text":"CLASSICALRADIUSFACTOR::Float64 - Classical radius factor derived from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.CLASSICAL_RADIUS_FACTOR"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.K_BOLTZMANN","page":"API Reference","text":"KBOLTZMANN::Float64 - Boltzmann constant kB in eV/K per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.K_BOLTZMANN"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.EPS_0","page":"API Reference","text":"EPS_0::Float64 - Permittivity of free space in 1/(eV*m) per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.EPS_0"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_0","page":"API Reference","text":"MU_0::Float64 - Vacuum permeability in eV*s^2/m per the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.MU_0"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.AVOGADRO","page":"API Reference","text":"AVOGADRO::Float64 - Avogadro's constant number/mol (exact)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.AVOGADRO"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.FINE_STRUCTURE","page":"API Reference","text":"FINE_STRUCTURE::Float64 - Finse structure constant from the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.FINE_STRUCTURE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.RELEASE_YEAR","page":"API Reference","text":"RELEASE_YEAR::Int - The release year of the currently selected CODATA values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.RELEASE_YEAR"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.KG_PER_AMU","page":"API Reference","text":"KGPERAMU::Float64 - Kilograms per Dalton conversion in the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.KG_PER_AMU"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.EV_PER_AMU","page":"API Reference","text":"EVPERAMU::Float64 - eV/c^2 per Dalton conversion in the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.EV_PER_AMU"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.J_PER_EV","page":"API Reference","text":"JPEREV::Float64 - Joules per eV in the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.J_PER_EV"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.EV_PER_J","page":"API Reference","text":"EVPERJ::Float64 - eV per Joule in the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.EV_PER_J"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_PER_EV","page":"API Reference","text":"GPEREV::Float64 - Grams per eV/c^2 in the selected CODATA release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.G_PER_EV"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.KG_PER_MEV_C2","page":"API Reference","text":"KGPERMEV_C2::Float64 - Kilograms per MeV/c^2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.KG_PER_MEV_C2"},{"category":"section","location":"man/species/#man-species","page":"Species","text":"A Species is the central data structure of AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.jl. It bundles all intrinsic properties of a particle — mass, charge, spin, magnetic moment, and particle kind — into a single immutable object.","title":"Species"},{"category":"section","location":"man/species/#Constructing-a-species","page":"Species","text":"All species are created with the single-string constructor\n\nSpecies(speciesname::String)\n\nThe string format encodes the particle identity, isotope (for atoms), and charge state in one compact expression.","title":"Constructing a species"},{"category":"section","location":"man/species/#Subatomic-particles","page":"Species","text":"Pass the openPMD particle name exactly as it appears in the table below.\n\nString Particle\n\"electron\" electron\n\"positron\" positron\n\"proton\" proton\n\"anti-proton\" antiproton\n\"neutron\" neutron\n\"anti-neutron\" antineutron\n\"muon\" muon (μ⁻)\n\"anti-muon\" antimuon (μ⁺)\n\"pion0\" neutral pion\n\"pion+\" positive pion\n\"pion-\" negative pion\n\"deuteron\" deuteron\n\"anti-deuteron\" antideuteron\n\"triton\" triton\n\"anti-triton\" antitriton\n\"helion\" helion\n\"anti-helion\" antihelion\n\"photon\" photon\n\ne  = Species(\"electron\")\nmu = Species(\"muon\")\npi0 = Species(\"pion0\")","title":"Subatomic particles"},{"category":"section","location":"man/species/#Atomic-species","page":"Species","text":"Atomic symbols from \"H\" (Z = 1) through \"Og\" (Z = 118) are supported.\n\nThe full format for an atomic species string is:\n\n[mass_number] symbol [charge]\n\nwhere both mass_number and charge are optional.\n\nMass number — when given as ASCII digits it must be preceded by #; it may also be written with Unicode superscript digits (no # needed). A bare ASCII mass number such as \"4He\" is not accepted:\n\nSpecies(\"He\")    # helium, abundance-averaged mass\nSpecies(\"#4He\")  # helium-4  (# prefix is required for ASCII digits)\nSpecies(\"⁴He\")   # helium-4  (Unicode superscript)\n\nCharge state — appended after the symbol:\n\nSyntax Meaning\n\"Li+\" +1\n\"Li++\" +2\n\"Li+++\" +3\n\"Li+3\" +3\n\"K-\" −1\n\"K--\" −2\n\"K-3\" −3\n\"N⁻³\" −3 (Unicode superscript magnitude)\n\nUp to three repeated + or - signs are accepted; for larger charges use the +n / -n form.\n\nSpecies(\"Li+++\") == Species(\"Li+3\")   # true\nSpecies(\"K---\")  == Species(\"K-3\")    # true","title":"Atomic species"},{"category":"section","location":"man/species/#Anti-atoms","page":"Species","text":"Prepend \"anti-\" to any atomic symbol:\n\nSpecies(\"anti-H\")   # antihydrogen\nSpecies(\"anti-Fe\")  # anti-iron","title":"Anti-atoms"},{"category":"section","location":"man/species/#Null-species","page":"Species","text":"A null (placeholder) species is created with no arguments or with the strings \"Null\", \"null\", or \"\":\n\nSpecies()        # null species\nSpecies(\"null\")  # also null\n\nUse isnullspecies to test for a null species.\n\n","title":"Null species"},{"category":"section","location":"man/species/#Accessor-functions","page":"Species","text":"See the API Reference for full docstrings of all accessor functions.\n\n","title":"Accessor functions"},{"category":"section","location":"man/species/#The-Kind-enum","page":"Species","text":"Every species carries a kind field of type Kind.T (provided by EnumX.jl):\n\nValue Meaning\nKind.LEPTON electron, positron, muon, anti-muon\nKind.HADRON proton, neutron, pions, deuteron, …\nKind.PHOTON photon\nKind.ATOM any atomic / ionic species\nKind.NULL null / placeholder species\n\nkindof(Species(\"electron\")) == Kind.LEPTON   # true\nkindof(Species(\"Fe\"))       == Kind.ATOM     # true\n\n","title":"The Kind enum"},{"category":"section","location":"man/species/#Internal-data-dictionaries","page":"Species","text":"Two dictionaries back the species constructor and are exported for advanced use.\n\nSee the API Reference for full docstrings of all internal reference dictionaries.","title":"Internal data dictionaries"},{"category":"section","location":"man/constants/#man-constants","page":"Constants","text":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.jl exports a flat set of const values drawn from the active CODATA release.  All values are Float64 scalars in the units shown below.\n\nThe active release is selected at package-load time via a Preferences.jl setting (default: 2022).  See CODATA Releases for how to change it.\n\n","title":"Physical Constants"},{"category":"section","location":"man/constants/#Particle-masses","page":"Constants","text":"Units: eV/c²\n\nConstant Particle\nM_ELECTRON electron\nM_PROTON proton\nM_NEUTRON neutron\nM_MUON muon\nM_DEUTERON deuteron\nM_HELION helion (³He nucleus)\nM_TRITON triton (³H nucleus) ‡\nM_PION_0 neutral pion †\nM_PION_CHARGED charged pion †\n\n† Pion masses are taken from the Particle Data Group (PDG), not from CODATA, and are the same across all release years.\n\n‡ M_TRITON is not tabulated in the 2002 CODATA release; CODATA2002.M_TRITON is NaN.\n\n","title":"Particle masses"},{"category":"section","location":"man/constants/#Magnetic-dipole-moments","page":"Constants","text":"Units: eV/T\n\nStored values are CODATA SI values (J/T) converted to eV/T via EV_PER_J.\n\nConstant Particle\nMU_ELECTRON electron\nMU_PROTON proton\nMU_NEUTRON neutron\nMU_MUON muon\nMU_DEUTERON deuteron\nMU_HELION helion\nMU_TRITON triton\n\n","title":"Magnetic dipole moments"},{"category":"section","location":"man/constants/#Spin-g-factors-(dimensionless)","page":"Constants","text":"Constant Particle CODATA availability\nG_ELECTRON electron all releases\nG_PROTON proton all releases\nG_NEUTRON neutron all releases\nG_MUON muon all releases\nG_DEUTERON deuteron all releases §\nG_HELION helion 2010 and later §\nG_TRITON triton all releases §\n\n§ These three are not the raw CODATA values — see below.","title":"Spin g-factors (dimensionless)"},{"category":"section","location":"man/constants/#Composite-nucleus-g-factors-are-renormalized","page":"Constants","text":"CODATA/NIST tabulates the deuteron, helion, and triton g-factors relative to the nuclear magneton mu_N = ehbar  2m_p, i.e. the published number is\n\ng_textNIST = fracmuImu_N\n\nso the proton mass — not the particle's own mass — sets the scale.  Used directly in a = (g-2)2 those values give a meaningless anomaly (for the deuteron, (0857ldots - 2)2).\n\nThis package instead stores the g-factor in the convention boldsymbolmu = gfrace2mmathbfS, where the particle's own mass sets the scale, which is the convention the spin-precession (Thomas–BMT) equation and the gyromagnetic anomaly assume.  Converting between the two is a single mass ratio, applied at package-load time:\n\ng = g_textNISTfracmm_p\n\nExported constant Definition\nG_DEUTERON G_DEUTERON_NUCLEAR * M_DEUTERON / M_PROTON\nG_HELION G_HELION_NUCLEAR * M_HELION / M_PROTON\nG_TRITON G_TRITON_NUCLEAR * M_TRITON / M_PROTON\n\nThe _NUCLEAR suffix marks the nuclear-magneton normalization: those are the names the raw NIST values carry as fields of the release structs (CODATA2022.G_DEUTERON_NUCLEAR, …).  There is no exported top-level G_*_NUCLEAR constant; the active release's values are held as the internal AtomicAndPhysicalConstants._G_DEUTERON, ._G_HELION, and ._G_TRITON.\n\nThe mass ratio uses the mass from the same active CODATA release as the g-factor, so switching releases with set_release renormalizes consistently.\n\nOnly these three struct fields carry the _NUCLEAR suffix.  The electron, muon, proton, and neutron g-factors need no rescaling — CODATA already tabulates them against the magneton built from the particle's own mass — so G_ELECTRON, G_MUON, G_PROTON, and G_NEUTRON keep their plain names in the release structs and are exported exactly as published.\n\nBecause the renormalization is folded into the constants themselves, g_spin and gyromagnetic_anomaly need no special-casing — a = (g-2)2 applies uniformly to every subatomic species:\n\ngyromagnetic_anomaly(Species(\"deuteron\"))   # ≈ -0.1429872697\n\n","title":"Composite-nucleus g-factors are renormalized"},{"category":"section","location":"man/constants/#Gyromagnetic-anomalies-(dimensionless)","page":"Constants","text":"The gyromagnetic anomaly is defined as a = (g - 2)2. gyromagnetic_anomaly takes the unsigned g-factor — the default of g_spin — so particles with a negative stored g-factor (electron, muon, neutron, helion) come out with the conventional positive anomaly rather than a value near -2.\n\nConstant Particle CODATA availability\nANOMALY_ELECTRON electron 2010 and later\nANOMALY_MUON muon 2010 and later\n\n","title":"Gyromagnetic anomalies (dimensionless)"},{"category":"section","location":"man/constants/#Other-physical-constants","page":"Constants","text":"Constant Description Units\nE_CHARGE elementary charge C\nC_LIGHT speed of light m/s\nH_PLANCK Planck's constant h eV·s\nH_BAR reduced Planck constant ħ eV·s\nR_ELECTRON classical electron radius m\nR_PROTON classical proton radius m\nCLASSICAL_RADIUS_FACTOR e^2  (4pivarepsilon_0) = r_e m_e c^2 † eV·m\nK_BOLTZMANN Bolzmann's constant k<sub>B<sub> eV/K\nEPS_0 permittivity of free space 1/(eV·m)\nMU_0 vacuum permeability eV·s²/m\nAVOGADRO Avogadro's constant mol⁻¹\nFINE_STRUCTURE fine-structure constant dimensionless\nRELEASE_YEAR active CODATA release year —\n\n† CLASSICAL_RADIUS_FACTOR is not a tabulated CODATA value; it is computed as R_ELECTRON * M_ELECTRON from the active release.  It is the same for all particles of charge ±1.  Because it is derived, it is not a field of the CODATA_release structs (e.g. there is no CODATA2022.CLASSICAL_RADIUS_FACTOR).\n\n","title":"Other physical constants"},{"category":"section","location":"man/constants/#Unit-conversion-constants","page":"Constants","text":"Constant Conversion\nKG_PER_AMU kg per dalton\nEV_PER_AMU eV/c² per dalton\nJ_PER_EV joules per eV\nEV_PER_J eV per joule\nG_PER_EV grams per eV/c²\nKG_PER_MEV_C2 kg per MeV/c²","title":"Unit-conversion constants"},{"category":"section","location":"#AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.jl","page":"Home","text":"(Image: Stable) (Image: Dev) (Image: Build Status)\n\nAtomicAndPhysicalConstants.jl provides fundamental physical constants and particle data drawn from official CODATA releases (2002 – 2022).  Values are expressed in units natural to accelerator and atomic physics (eV, eV/c², eV·s, eV/T, …).","title":"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"AtomicAndPhysicalConstants\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using AtomicAndPhysicalConstants\n\n# ── physical constants ──────────────────────────────────────────────\nC_LIGHT    # 2.99792458e8  m/s\nH_PLANCK   # 4.135667696e-15  eV·s\nM_ELECTRON # 510998.95069  eV/c²\n\n# ── particle species ────────────────────────────────────────────────\ne    = Species(\"electron\")\np    = Species(\"proton\")\nh    = Species(\"H\")          # neutral hydrogen, abundance-averaged mass\nhe3  = Species(\"#3He\")       # neutral helium-3\nhion = Species(\"H+\")         # singly-ionised hydrogen\nantip = Species(\"anti-proton\")\n\n# ── accessor functions ───────────────────────────────────────────────\nmassof(e)                    # 510998.95069  eV/c²\nmassof(h, AMU=true)          # mass in atomic mass units\nchargeof(hion)               # 1  (units of e)\nspinof(e)                    # 0.5  ħ\ng_spin(e)                    # 2.00231930436092  (unsigned; signed=true for -2.0023…)\ngyromagnetic_anomaly(e)      # ≈ 0.00115965…  = (|g| - 2)/2\nmomentof(p)                  # magnetic dipole moment  eV/T\niso_of(he3)                  # 3  (mass number)","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Package-overview","page":"Home","text":"Topic Description\nSpecies Constructing and querying particle species\nConstants Exported physical constants\nCODATA Releases Switching between CODATA release years\nAPI Reference Complete docstring index","title":"Package overview"}]
}
